From 3eab5fd752b1fa73a48357f1ed7bdf251074e533 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gauthier Piarrette <gauthierpiarrette@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 14:18:39 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] SMC: set a page's blocks aside at a write fault and restore
 them when its instruction bytes are unchanged

An SMC write fault on a page erased every block compiled from that page (InvalidationTracker ->
InvalidateRange, the whole page) and the next execution recompiled them all. Microsoft Flight
Simulator 2024 writes data that shares pages with hot code all the time, so the page alternated
compile and protect, write and fault and erase, execute and miss and recompile: per-thread
counters on the M4 (2026-10-16) showed the instruction bytes unchanged at almost every
re-protection (idle: 1,012 unchanged against 3 changed; loading: 5,136 against 0; 90 to 98 percent
of the compiles were recompiles), and the present-time spikes of a flight matched the seconds of
JIT bursts (spike seconds averaged 976 ms of JIT against 551, 651 SMC events against 228).

Now the fault side sets the page's blocks aside in a fixed table in the GuestToHostMap: their links
in are cut and they leave BlockList and CodePages (unreachable, as if erased), but the entries are
kept with a copy of the page and a mask of the instruction bytes they were decoded from (each block
records its instruction byte ranges at compile time). Nothing allocates on the fault side: the
handler holds the thread-creation and code-invalidation mutexes, and two diagnostic builds that
allocated there froze the game in a lock cycle through Wine's FLS critical section. Blocks that
span other pages are erased as before (a page copy vouches for one page). When a thread next needs
code on the page (CompileBlock after a lookup miss), the page is protected again first, then the
masked bytes are compared with the copy: unchanged puts the blocks back and returns the wanted one
without compiling; changed frees them. A page set aside again before a restoration drops the old
record; explicit invalidations (frees, unmaps, protection changes) drop every set-aside block that
touches their range, and cache clears drop the table.

Measured on the M4, one flight each so within the run-to-run variance: the first flight parked at
27.4 fps with p99 frame time 57 ms against 25.7 fps and 118 ms without, roll 24.4 against 24.1,
15 present spikes over 120 ms against 50; two later flights sat inside the variance, where GPU
hangs set the spike count. Six counters appended to the per-thread statistics of FEX_PROFILESTATS=1
(ThreadStats grows to 160 bytes): recompiles, entries on the faulting pages, entries erased, pages
restored, pages changed, slot evictions.
